{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":38039504,"ns":0,"title":"Hochster–Roberts theorem","extract":"In algebra, the Hochster–Roberts theorem, introduced by Melvin Hochster and Joel L. Roberts in 1974, states that rings of invariants of linearly reductive groups acting on regular rings are Cohen–Macaulay.\nIn other words, if V is a rational representation of a linearly reductive group G over a field k, then there exist algebraically independent invariant homogeneous polynomials \n  \n    \n      \n        \n          f\n          \n            1\n          \n        \n        ,\n        ⋯\n        ,\n        \n          f\n          \n            d\n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle f_{1},\\cdots ,f_{d}}\n  \n such that \n  \n    \n      \n        k\n        [\n        V\n        \n          ]\n          \n            G\n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle k[V]^{G}}\n  \n is a free finite graded module over \n  \n    \n      \n        k\n        [\n        \n          f\n          \n            1\n          \n        \n        ,\n        ⋯\n        ,\n        \n          f\n          \n            d\n          \n        \n        ]\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle k[f_{1},\\cdots ,f_{d}]}\n  \n.","description":"Theorem in ring theory","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}